+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=5.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399406 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399407 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399408 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399409 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399410 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399410 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399405 1080 #uuid "25281d74-3b09-5c83-b4d4-d24247da42a5" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399406 1081 "$x=3$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399406 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399406 1084 #uuid "3099f3c2-846b-5ae3-b13d-62bf2853c8e6" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399407 1081 "$x=1$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399407 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399407 1084 #uuid "80bf0b4b-0794-5476-96ae-6da113dacb97" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399408 1081 "$x=3$ and $x=\\sqrt{5}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399408 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399408 1084 #uuid "1d2185e2-fc3d-5abf-9cc2-33c57fd4ee19" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399409 1081 "$x=1$ and $x=3$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399409 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399409 1084 #uuid "4278a543-dfc0-5d1f-b8c7-13dc3aeb961d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399410 1081 "$x=1$ and $x=5$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399410 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399410 1084 #uuid "7ea262a0-90f2-5ce8-828c-598aa9e08bf9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399412 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399411 1069 "q-99056" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399411 1071 "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399411 1072 #uuid "70a98919-dd4e-535e-b918-4ce91d1560b2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399411 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+6x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399413 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399414 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399415 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399416 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399417 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399414 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399412 1080 #uuid "dcf1d3ea-9362-58c4-b795-b8796b5019b7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399413 1081 "$x=2$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399413 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399413 1084 #uuid "065b8702-9c1f-5408-a110-0b6b1f931f94" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399414 1081 "$x=1$ and $x=2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399414 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399414 1084 #uuid "7e04b74f-d9c6-59e7-a8ee-004d2123563f" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399415 1081 "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399415 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399415 1084 #uuid "625ac297-0116-50dc-b32e-593a5c25fd5b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399416 1081 "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399416 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399416 1084 #uuid "2f10324a-c1cb-5f86-99bd-f736b0ed8050" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399417 1081 "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399417 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399417 1084 #uuid "acea3269-c2cc-5d39-87be-8ac3b32540bc" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399419 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399418 1069 "q-9926" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399418 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}=\\frac{x}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399418 1072 #uuid "fbe3ba98-4d51-53d7-a612-90e7db738fe7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399418 1074 "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2}|x| \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<1.$ So, the radius of convergence is $1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-1$ and $x=1$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.  So the endpoint $x=1$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=-1$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-1,1].$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399420 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399421 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399422 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399423 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399424 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399421 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399419 1080 #uuid "dc050dae-f829-533e-a886-22db5daf03b6" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399420 1081 "x\\in [-\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{2}{3}]" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399420 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399420 1084 #uuid "8f06d73f-7ac7-568b-9656-314a94895833" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399421 1081 "x\\in [-1,1]" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399421 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399421 1084 #uuid "ed1a8387-fe3e-5916-b4a6-ea4d748af438" 13194139533478 1759218604